# Q55: a killed tray no longer leaves backends or a wedged dongle (2026-11-01 01:30-01:45)

## the defect

`Stop-Process` / Task Manager on `DropBridge.exe` runs no cleanup. The children kept
running and kept 49876/49877/8771 bound, so the next start died with
"port 49876 is already in use"; and because the radio was killed mid-transfer, the
RTL8821CU was left wedged until a physical replug (the Q58 family).

## the fix, three layers

1. **Job object for every non-radio child** (`ChildJob`, kernel32 P/Invoke):
   `CreateJobObject` + `JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE`, and each child is assigned
   right after `Process.Start`. When the supervisor dies - including a force kill - the
   OS tears those children down with it, so no port and no orphan survives.
2. **The radio stays out of the job** and watches the supervisor instead: the supervisor
   passes `AIRBRIDGE_PARENT_PID`, the radio opens that process with `SYNCHRONIZE` and
   waits on it *and* `Local\DropBridgeRadioStop` with `WaitForMultipleObjects`. Either
   signal closes the listener, so `accept()` fails and the ordinary teardown runs
   (StopRxLoop -> bounded wait -> device Stop -> libusb release). Being in the job would
   TerminateProcess it mid-transfer, which is exactly what wedges the chip.
3. **Reclaim on start** (`ReclaimLeftovers`): when a port is already bound, the
   supervisor kills only processes whose executable sits under its own package root
   (radio, bridge, netd, awdld, python) and tries again. Any other program on that port
   is left alone and still reported as "port in use".

## measured

Start -> ready 01:43:14 (40 s), all six processes up. Then `Stop-Process -Id <tray> -Force`:

  after 8 s: no process from the package, no listener on 49876/49877/8771

Restart immediately, **no replug**:

  01:43:32  FW ready after 7,700 us / 1 poll
  01:44:14  radio listening (43 s to data plane)
  01:45:03  "shutdown: done" / "stopped (exit 0)"   <- that run's own auto-exit

So the chip came back clean on the first try, which is the outcome the whole Q58/Q55
line was after: **a crash costs neither a stuck port nor a cable pull.**

## note on log loss

The radio's own last WARN lines are written to the supervisor's log sink, so a
force-killed supervisor cannot record them. The proof of the clean radio shutdown is
therefore behavioural (the next start reaching the data plane with no replug), not a log
line - this is why the restart result above is the assertion.

## fixed at the same time (found while testing)

`HalmacJaguar2Fw`'s FW-boot poll was bounded by 20000 *polls*, on the assumption that a
poll costs 50 us. A stalled control transfer costs ~15 ms, so the budget became 309 s,
and a bring-up hung for five minutes before the supervisor gave up (measured 308,950,607
us in session 20261101-013606). It is now bounded by **wall clock: 1 s**, which keeps the
original intent (absorb a slow MCU) without the unbounded hang.
